# Files that are executed inside a Linux container must arrive with LF endings.
#
# **This is not the `.gitattributes` CLAUDE.md tells you not to add.** That instruction is about
# silencing the `LF will be replaced by CRLF` warning on every commit, and that warning is still
# expected and still not silenced -- nothing here covers Python, TypeScript, Markdown or anything
# else. This file exists because `core.autocrlf` is `true` on this machine and a shell script is
# not text to a kernel: it is a program whose first line names its interpreter.
#
# Measured 2026-08-18, by cloning `main` from GitHub into a clean directory and running the result:
#
#     $ head -c 40 docker/entrypoint.sh | od -c
#     #   !   /   u   s   r   /   b   i   n   /   e   n   v       b   a   s   h  \r  \n
#
#     $ docker run --rm -v .../docker:/x python:3.12-slim /x/entrypoint.sh
#     env: 'bash\r': No such file or directory
#
# So the one-command install was broken for exactly the person it is written for -- somebody on
# Windows cloning the repository for the first time -- while working perfectly in the worktree it
# was built in, because that working copy happened to hold LF. A defect that only a fresh clone
# can show is why the ship plan asks for fresh-clone bring-up to be verified by somebody who did
# not build it.

*.sh text eol=lf
docker/entrypoint.sh text eol=lf

# BuildKit tolerates CRLF in most instructions and then does not in a heredoc or a line
# continuation. There is no reason to find out which on somebody else's machine.
Dockerfile text eol=lf
